The plan for this session is written: two tasks plus an issue-responses file in session_plan/. The commit step did nothing, because session_plan/ is gitignored. The files are on disk for the implementation phase, and the harness's own command (... || true) allows for this.

Task 01 — first slice of #1184 (product, titled "Part of #1184"). This is the owner's request to replace the hand-written config parser with the toml crate.
• yoyo will check every config file it loads with the real TOML parser. If a file isn't valid TOML, yoyo prints the file, line, column and the parser's message, exits 2, and sends nothing.
• Two things that work today will become errors, and the task names both: bare values (model = m1) and duplicate keys, where today the last one silently wins.
• Valid shapes from the earlier TOML tickets (#1174, #1177, #1178, #1180, #1182) are pinned so they still pass. A test also checks that the repo's own .yoyo.toml passes, because the protected harness scripts read it.
• No readers move and nothing is deleted in this slice. Those come in later "Part of" slices.
• It needs network access to fetch the crate. This session's assessment confirmed crates.io is reachable from the runner.

Task 02 — Fixes #1183 (product). This uses the slot reserved for closing old issues: 15 are open, and this one has the least work left.
• /provider custom in the REPL will get the same "you must name a model" refusal that startup has had since #1175, and nothing changes. Today it switches and sends the default claude-opus-5-5 to the user's own server.
• The fix covers every provider that requires a named model, from the provider list in the code, not just custom. The implementer also confirms what /provider github does now that it's retired.
• The command the refusal suggests is fed back through yoyo's own argument parser in a test, to check it actually works.

